      <div class="case-content">
        <section id="motivation">
          <h2>1. Why NoSQL (Firestore) hit an architectural wall in POS/ERP</h2>
          <p>
            Meson initially shipped on Firebase/Cloud Firestore for rapid prototyping, but scaling into a multi-branch ERP with double-entry accounting, multi-currency cash registers, BOM recipe inventory, and offline-first sync exposed four structural limits:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No engine-enforced relational invariants:</strong> Firestore lacks partial unique indexes to guarantee at the database layer that a station has at most one active cash session or an order has at most one approved payment.</li>
            <li><strong>Concurrent inventory race conditions:</strong> multiple POS stations and offline dispatches deducting the same SKU require explicit row locks (<code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>), exact integer/fixed-point arithmetic, and transactional idempotency receipts.</li>
            <li><strong>Document-read billing on ERP analytics:</strong> Kardex audit trails, Z-Close reconciliations, 3-way AP matching, and multi-branch reports suffer under per-document read pricing without native SQL joins and aggregations.</li>
            <li><strong>Fragmented access governance:</strong> collection-path security rules made uniform multi-tenant JWT enforcement (<code>entity_id</code>) alongside transactional server RPCs error-prone.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="execution">
          <h2>2. Phased migration across a 4,052-file Dart monorepo</h2>
          <p>
            The cutover progressed incrementally in git without halting product delivery (<code>6e9547e2</code> 50% → <code>983f683f</code> 80% → <code>b0050578</code> 90% → <code>75ba7326</code> customer app → <code>c55582cd</code> full switchover), totaling <strong>502 changed files (<code>+35,730 / −11,731</code> lines)</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Complete legacy removal:</strong> the active monorepo contains <strong><code>0</code> files referencing <code>cloud_firestore</code></strong>, <strong><code>324</code> Dart files wired to Supabase/PostgreSQL</strong>, and <strong><code>389</code> files backed by local Isar WAL</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Reproducible SQL DDL:</strong> <code>18</code> idempotent SQL scripts (<code>apps/meson_business/scripts/sql/</code> + <code>BUCO</code>) encoding <code>16</code> core migration tables, <code>59</code> RLS policies, <code>22</code> <code>ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY</code> statements, <code>9</code> PL/pgSQL RPCs, <code>17</code> indexes, and <code>4</code> triggers.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="outbox-sql">
          <h2>3. Outbox-safe SQL projections &amp; partial unique indexes</h2>
          <p>
            A classic pitfall when migrating an offline-first system to SQL is placing rigid foreign keys on tables fed by asynchronous outbox retries: if a payment arrives before its parent cash session after a network partition, a strict <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> deadlocks or drops valid offline mutations.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Order-independent cloud projections (<code>06_cash_register_financial_sync.sql</code>):</strong> local Isar remains the offline operational authority; <code>cash_sessions</code>, <code>cash_movements</code>, <code>pos_payments</code>, and <code>cash_currency_balances</code> use client-generated UUID keys and logical references without rigid FKs so retried outbox entries converge regardless of arrival order.</li>
            <li><strong>PostgreSQL-enforced financial invariants:</strong>
              <ul>
                <li><code>cash_sessions_one_active_per_station</code>: partial unique index on <code>(entity_id, station_id) WHERE status IN ('open', 'closing')</code>.</li>
                <li><code>pos_payments_one_approved_per_order</code>: partial unique index on <code>(entity_id, order_id) WHERE state = 'approved'</code> plus <code>idempotency_key TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE</code>.</li>
                <li>Exact-cent <code>BIGINT</code> positive constraints (<code>&gt; 0</code>) and strict 3-character ISO-4217 currency checks (<code>char_length = 3</code>).</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="inventory">
          <h2>4. Concurrent inventory evolution: Delta → PN-Counter CRDT → Contract v1</h2>
          <div class="paper-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="en-p11-evol">
            <table class="paper-table">
              <caption id="en-p11-evol">Table 1 · Three real iterations of Meson's transactional stock engine in PostgreSQL</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Stage / Script</th>
                  <th scope="col">SQL Mechanism</th>
                  <th scope="col">Failure Mode Eliminated</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Stage 1 · <code>01_atomic_inventory_and_modifiers.sql</code></th>
                  <td data-label="Mechanism">Immutable Kardex ledger (<code>inventory_transactions</code> with <code>mutation_id UNIQUE</code>) + <code>apply_inventory_stock_delta</code> RPC + dropping obsolete overloads.</td>
                  <td data-label="Eliminates">Eliminates Firestore lost-update races and PostgREST HTTP <code>300 Multiple Choices</code> overload ambiguity.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Stage 2 · <code>02_inventory_pn_counter_crdt.sql</code></th>
                  <td data-label="Mechanism"><code>inventory_crdt_nodes (item_id, node_id)</code> table + <code>merge_inventory_crdt_state</code> RPC using Join-Semilattice pointwise <code>GREATEST()</code> LUB and global stock <code>SUM(p_val) − SUM(n_val)</code>.</td>
                  <td data-label="Eliminates">Guarantees idempotent convergence across offline multi-station retries and out-of-order state syncs.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">Stage 3 · <code>08_stock_dispatch_contract.sql</code></th>
                  <td data-label="Mechanism"><code>apply_inventory_stock_dispatch_v1</code> acquiring <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> before receipt checks, fixed-point <code>BIGINT × 10,000</code> scaling (≤ <code>2⁵³−1</code>), dedicated transport receipts (<code>inventory_stock_dispatch_receipts</code>) + <code>SHA-256 dispatch_digest</code> verification (<code>23505</code> on payload mismatch), and <code>REVOKE EXECUTE</code> on legacy scalar RPC.</td>
                  <td data-label="Eliminates">Eliminates TOCTOU double-deduction races, prevents Kardex double-counting, and blocks unsafe downgrade fallback to legacy clients.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="rls">
          <h2>5. Zero-trust multi-tenant RLS &amp; Supabase Linter hardening</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Signed-JWT tenant isolation:</strong> every financial and stock table enforces <code>entity_id = (auth.jwt() -&gt;&gt; 'entity_id')</code>; <code>SECURITY DEFINER</code> RPCs pin <code>SET search_path = pg_catalog, public, pg_temp</code> and explicitly verify <code>auth.uid()</code>, <code>auth.role()</code>, and tenant claim match, raising <code>SQLSTATE 42501</code> on scope violations.</li>
            <li><strong>Supabase Linter remediation (<code>fix_supabase_linter_warnings.sql</code>):</strong> replaced permissive public write policies across <code>change_logs</code>, <code>chat_rooms_typing</code>, <code>deletion_logs</code>, <code>marketplace_order_items</code>, <code>radar_shot_likes</code>, and <code>reviews</code>, converted <code>sync_employee_photos()</code> to <code>SECURITY INVOKER</code>, revoked <code>PUBLIC</code>/<code>anon</code> function grants, and removed public bucket listing exposure on <code>storage.objects</code> (<code>app_releases</code>).</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="tradeoffs">
          <h2>6. Architectural trade-offs &amp; threats to validity</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Deliberate FK omission on outbox projections:</strong> omitting strict foreign keys on cloud projection tables prevents offline retry deadlocks at the cost of enforcing cross-table referential validation in domain reconcilers and automated tests.</li>
            <li><strong>Git-to-Staging deployment drift (audited in <a href="/en/lab/meson-baas-rls-telemetry-audit/">Paper 18</a>):</strong> while all 18 hardened SQL scripts (Stages 2–3: PN-Counter CRDT, <code>cash_sessions</code>, <code>apply_inventory_stock_dispatch_v1</code>, and 59 JWT-scoped policies) and 324 Supabase Dart modules are verified in the monorepo with zero Firestore remnants, a follow-up live catalog audit of the pre-production instance (<code>112</code> tables, <code>59</code> migrations, <code>411</code> policies) confirmed deployment drift: staging still runs Stage-1 <code>apply_inventory_stock_delta</code>, and paired pre/post Firestore vs. PostgreSQL latency/billing logs were not archived prior to cutover.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
